<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1898 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">'O sole mio</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from Naples, written in 1898, whose title means &quot;my sun&quot;. Its tune is sung and played all over the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Giovanni Capurro</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Eduardo di Capua and Alfredo Mazzucchi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Neapolitan song</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Naples, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1898</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Giovanni Capurro (1898): in the public domain in the United States (published before 1930); in Italy protected until about 2042, since a ruling of 2002 named Alfredo Mazzucchi (died 1972) co-composer)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a>&quot; is a Neapolitan song written in 1898. Its words, in the Neapolitan language, are by Giovanni Capurro; the music is by Eduardo di Capua (1865–1917) and Alfredo Mazzucchi (1878–1972). The title means &quot;my sun&quot; or &quot;my sunshine&quot;.</p><p>For nearly 75 years after it was published, the music was credited to di Capua alone. The traditional account had him compose it in April 1898 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, while he was touring with his father's band. In fact the melody was worked up from one of 23 that he had bought from Mazzucchi the year before. In November 1972, shortly after her father's death, Mazzucchi's daughter lodged a declaration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s Office of Literary, Artistic and Scientific Property, asking that he be recognised as co-composer of 18 of di Capua's songs, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a>&quot; among them. In October 2002 Maria Alvau, a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, upheld it: the songs contained melodies Mazzucchi had composed and sold to di Capua in June 1897, with written permission to use them freely. As a result, the song had not yet entered the public domain in the countries of the Berne Convention, as it would have done in Italy had di Capua been its only composer.</p><p>The words have been translated into other languages. Charles W. Harrison recorded the first English version in 1915, and in 1921 William E. Booth-Clibborn set a hymn, &quot;Down from His Glory&quot;, to the tune. In 1949 Tony Martin recorded &quot;There's No Tomorrow&quot;, new English words by Al Hoffman, Leo Corday and Leon Carr on the same melody. About ten years later Elvis Presley, then serving with the US Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, heard that record; after his discharge he asked for words of his own, which Aaron Schroeder and Wally Gold wrote as &quot;It's Now or Never&quot;, a worldwide hit. In concerts of the mid-1970s Presley explained where the song came from and had Sherrill Nielsen sing part of the Neapolitan original first.</p><p>The song has been recorded by many singers, among them Enrico Caruso, Beniamino Gigli, Rosa Ponselle, Mario Lanza, Luciano Pavarotti and Andrea Bocelli; Pavarotti's performance won the 1980 Grammy Award for Best Classical Vocal Performance. At the opening ceremony of the 1920 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> it was played in place of the Italian national anthem, whose sheet music had not reached the band. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 1980s its melody carried the jingle of a series of television advertisements for Cornetto ice cream.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Neapolitan love song built around the image named in its title, the singer's own sun.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Neapolitan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Neapolitan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Che bella cosa è na jurnata ’e sole,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What a beautiful thing a sunny day is,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">n’aria serena doppo na tempesta!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the calm air after a storm!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Pe’ ll’aria fresca pare già na festa...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the fresh air it already feels like a feast...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Che bella cosa na jurnata ’e sole.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What a beautiful thing a sunny day is.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma n’atu sole cchiù bello, oi ne’,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But another sun, more beautiful, my dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole mio sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my own sun, is in your face!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole, ’o sole mio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sun, my own sun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sta nfronte a te, sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is in your face, is in your face!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Luceno ’e llastre d’’a fenesta toia;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The panes of your window shine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’na lavannara canta e se ne vanta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a washerwoman sings and boasts of it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">e pe’ tramente torce, spanne e canta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and while she wrings, hangs out and sings,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">luceno ’e llastre d’’a fenesta toia.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the panes of your window shine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma n’atu sole cchiù bello, oi ne’,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But another sun, more beautiful, my dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole mio sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my own sun, is in your face!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole, ’o sole mio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sun, my own sun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sta nfronte a te, sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is in your face, is in your face!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Quanno fa notte e ’o sole se ne scenne,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When night falls and the sun goes down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">me vene quasi ’na malincunia;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>something like melancholy comes over me;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sotta ’a fenesta toia restarria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I would stay beneath your window</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">quanno fa notte e ’o sole se ne scenne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when night falls and the sun goes down.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma n’atu sole cchiù bello, oi ne’,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But another sun, more beautiful, my dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole mio sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my own sun, is in your face!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">’o sole, ’o sole mio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sun, my own sun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sta nfronte a te, sta nfronte a te!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is in your face, is in your face!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Giovanni Capurro (1898). Text from <a href="https://nap.wikisource.org/wiki/'O_sole_mio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; in the public domain in the United States.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>The words are in Neapolitan, not Italian: &quot;'o sole mio&quot; is &quot;my sun&quot;, &quot;nfronte a te&quot; &quot;on your face&quot; (literally, on your forehead).</li><li>The words are in the public domain in the United States, where they were published before 1930; in Italy the song is protected until about 2042, since a court ruled in 2002 that Alfredo Mazzucchi, who died in 1972, wrote the music with Eduardo di Capua.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
